  "body": "## What changed\n\n- New detector `camcraft_motion::multiaxis::axis_helix::helix`. It finds a helical surface (a modelled screw thread) from the facet normal lines. It gives the axis, the pitch and the hand.\n- Threading now refuses a model with a helical thread. The message names the helical thread, its pitch and its hand. It tells the user to model the thread as revolved V grooves.\n- The refusal is in the kernel (`turning::helical_refusal`, called by `thread_planned_in`). It is also in the app Threading arm, before the slice of the job region.\n- `axis.rs`: the facet-datum loop and the normal moment move into two helpers (`data_of`, `moment`). The axis fit does not change.\n\n## Cause\n\nThreading reads a modelled thread as a revolved V section. A helical thread has no such section. Two earlier steps refused the issue's part with messages that named other causes:\n\n- STEP route: the slice of the job region said `the mesh is open ... dangling chord ends`.\n- Native route: the axis fit did not accept the helical flanks and said `every axis fits this model equally`. When the axis fit passes, the kernel said `nothing to thread ... threading cuts a MODELED thread`, and that does not tell the user that the thread is helical.\n\n## How the detector works\n\nAbout a candidate axis `(o, u)`, each inclined facet gives the screw parameter `h = (n x u) . (o - p) / (n . u)`. The normal lines of a helical surface belong to a linear line complex (Pottmann & Wallner, Computational Line Geometry). A revolve gives `h = 0`. The two flanks of a helical thread give one common nonzero `h`. The gates are:\n\n- The agreeing area is at least half of the inclined area and at least 2% of the part.\n- The agreeing facets go all the way round the axis (the coarseness skip of `axis.rs`).\n\nThe pitch is the area-weighted mean of the agreeing band. The two triangles of one twisted quad read `h` low and high (1.356 and 1.665 for a 1.5 pitch), so the median would be about 10% low.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe QA files of the issue were not on this machine. The pins use a procedural shaft of the same shape: an M16x1.5 helical V, a Ø24 body and a Ø30 head, 70 long.\n\n- `camcraft-motion` `turning_thread_helical`:\n  - `a_helical_thread_is_refused_by_name`\n  - `a_left_hand_helical_thread_says_left_hand`\n  - `a_coarse_helical_thread_is_refused_by_name` (48 segments)\n  - `an_open_helical_mesh_is_refused_by_name` (facets removed)\n  - `a_revolved_v_thread_and_a_smooth_bar_are_not_helical` (the pitch is found to 2%)\n- `camcraft-app` `turning_thread_helical`:\n  - `a_helical_thread_is_refused_by_name_before_the_slice` (an open mesh, as the STEP route)\n\nMutation results:\n\n- The upstream kernel (`thread.rs`, `turning/mod.rs`) makes the four motion refusal pins fail with `nothing to thread - no valley 0.920 down ... threading cuts a MODELED thread` or `no material crosses the station interval`. My code makes them pass.\n- The upstream `generate.rs` (no app guard) makes the app pin fail with `M16: the source model cannot be sliced: the mesh is open at z = 2.1875: 12 dangling chord ends`. This is the message in the issue. My code makes it pass.\n\nGate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` is green (160 steps, 0 failed). This includes all the `turning_thread*` suites, and they keep their revolved-V fixtures. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean. After the gate run, I changed only lints (`is_multiple_of`, a collapsed `if let`). Then I ran the two pin files again (green) and clippy again (clean).\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nNone.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The issue's real STEP and `.sc` models (`/home/mikekinney/qa/runs/20261120-q03-turn-shaft/`). The files were not on this machine. A reviewer on skull can run `open_project` and `generate {name:\"M16Modeled\"}` / `generate {name:\"M16Native\"}` and expect the `HELICAL` refusal.\n- A reader for helical threads (a phase from the helix) is not part of this PR. It is new algorithm work and changes posted G-code.\n- This PR does not repair the STEP tessellation gaps of helical faces. That is an importer defect. Threading now refuses before the slice.\n- No heavy lane ran.\n\n## Overlaps\n\n- Open PRs that change `crates/app/src/operation/generate.rs` in other functions: #3059, #3058, #2997, #2920, #2890, #2836, #2831, #2822, #2716, #2714. None changes the Threading arm. Touches the same file, different function; whichever merges second refreshes.\n- `crates/motion/src/multiaxis/axis.rs` grows from 411 to 422 lines. The cause is the two extracted helpers. The file was already above 400 lines before this PR.\n\nDecision record: `docs/decisions/3023-helical-thread-refusal.md`.\n\nEffort: high\n\nChangelog: Threading refuses a model with a helical modelled thread with a message that names the helical thread, its pitch and its hand, in place of a slice or axis-fit error.\n\nFixes #3023\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
